# Alicloud Function Compute bundle — shared deps + the alibaba SDKs.
# Compiled to requirements-alicloud.txt. Nothing on the current branch
# reads this file (the alicloud deploy is a Phase 5 follow-up), but the
# dependency surface lives here so a future alicloud-deploy PR only has
# to flip LAMBDA_REQUIREMENTS_FILE / var.lambda_requirements to
# "requirements-alicloud.txt" without re-figuring-out which SDKs we
# depend on.
#
# `oss2` re-exports under both `oss2` and `alibabacloud_oss2`
# namespaces (the legacy short name is the only PyPI package). OOS /
# ECS use Alibaba's per-product SDK naming.
#
# The secret store is the OOS Parameter Store
# (`alibabacloud_oos20190601`), not KMS Secrets Manager: on KMS 3.0 a
# secret can only be created inside a dedicated KMS instance, and a
# KMS instance is a billable resource. The KMS SDK is therefore not
# needed at runtime — the CMK that encrypts the parameters is only an
# id we pass to OOS.
-r requirements.in

oss2
alibabacloud_oos20190601
alibabacloud_ecs20140526

# The Function Compute runtime's glibc is older than the one on the
# build machine: cryptography's manylinux_2_34 wheels (v44 and up)
# need GLIBC_2.33 and the FC runtime does not have it — the import
# dies with "version `GLIBC_2.33' not found (required by
# /code/cryptography/hazmat/bindings/_rust.abi3.so)". 43.0.3 is the
# newest release whose x86_64 wheels top out at manylinux_2_28.
# The AWS bundle keeps the shared pin (the Lambda runtime is
# AL2023, whose glibc is new enough).
cryptography<44
